      "id": "bbc-2026-08-15-us-women-in-politics-spark-conversation-on-motherhood",
      "title": "US women in politics spark conversation on motherhood",
      "summary": "The White House press secretary quit this week to be with her children, while Democrat Alexandra Ocasio-Cortez said she was freezing her eggs.",
      "body": "Last summer, Representative Sara Jacobs would run up to her office to give herself injections of hormones before rushing back to Congress for a session with her fellow lawmakers.The shots were for the second round of freezing her eggs, a medical procedure that can give women more time to have children.\"It was an empowering decision that gave me more agency,\" the 37-year-old said, adding that the process was challenging.\"Your body hurts. Your hormones are crazy. It's kind of like puberty and menopause at the same time.\"Jacobs announced she was beginning the egg freezing process in 2021, at a time when she said few lawmakers were discussing the topic in the halls of Congress. The process - which can cost more than $10,000 (£7,400) and is rarely covered by insurance - involves doctors harvesting eggs from the ovaries and freezing them for a future pregnancy.This week, Democratic Representative Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez announced she too was beginning the process of freezing her eggs, documenting the daily mechanics of giving herself hormone shots on social media.Seema Patel, an associate professor of law at UC Law San Francisco, also stays up late at night to try to finish her work so she doesn't have to miss moments with her children.\"The reality is that these years in your kids' lives are so fleeting, and I'm super aware of the time,\" she said.Patel worked for San Francisco's Office of Labor Standards Enforcement, which became the first local government in the US to pass a law requiring employers to provide paid parental leave.But through her career as a workers' rights lawyer, she has seen many lower-wage workers and parents who are not lucky enough to have access to paid time off.When Patel began working as a federal attorney, her boss advised her to save up her vacation days in case she eventually wanted to have children and needed to take leave. She ended up taking the advice.\"I was a brand new, young attorney who had not even contemplated that stage of my life, but I had to in that moment, face that question of - I better be saving my vacation days,\" she said.But even with some maternity leave and a dedicated partner, as her children - now ages 13, 11 and seven - have grown older, the work-life juggling act remains a challenge.\"I have a high-producing job,\" she said. \"How can I do that while also being there for my kids and attentive to them? I don't have an answer.\"For now, Jacobs, who has completed two rounds of egg freezing, plans to hold off on having children while her job requires her to fly across the country from California to Washington.\"Figuring out how to be a mum when I'm gone half the time across the country, it's logistically complicated,\" she said.Jacobs said she was impressed by her fellow lawmakers who have managed to make it work. Only a handful of women have given birth while in Congress, while a few dozen lawmakers are mothers to children under the age of 18.The federal government, like many US employers, does not offer paid parental leave, Jacobs noted.\"Both Congress and the general American workplace were designed by and for men who had often stay-at-home spouses who handled all of the domestic work,\" she said. \"It's going to take a lot of work for us to create a society where the domestic work is really shared equally between women and men.\"In the meantime, Jacobs feels reassured to have a back-up plan for whenever she does feel she can fit children into her busy career.\"I hope to be able to do that one day, but I just haven't figured the time is right just yet.\"",
