      "id": "bbc-2026-08-15-eleven-killed-in-israeli-strikes-on-southern-lebanon-authori",
      "title": "Eleven killed in Israeli strikes on southern Lebanon, authorities say",
      "summary": "Israel's military said it had struck \"Hezbollah terror infrastructure\" in response to what it described as earlier action against its soldiers.",
      "body": "Eleven people have been killed in Israeli strikes in southern Lebanon, authorities say, in one of the deadliest days of attacks since an agreement aimed at reducing hostilities was announced in June.Lebanon's prime minister said seven people were killed in a strike on a house on the outskirts of Ansar, 70km (43 miles) south of the capital, Beirut. State media reported three children and two women were among the dead.Israel's Defense Forces (IDF) said it had killed a commander of Hezbollah - the Iran-backed Shia militia - following an attack against Israeli soldiers.The commander's family \"who he used as human shields\" were present and \"harmed\", it added.Later on Saturday, another round of strikes hit the town of Deir al-Zahrani, killing four and wounding five others, the Lebanese ministry of health said.Lebanese authorities say the strike in Ansar at around 04:30 (01:30 GMT) occurred where displaced families were living. Footage on social media shows large plumes of smoke permeating through the streets of the village as sirens ring out.Lebanon's Prime Minister Nawaf Salam condemned the attacks, saying the seven civilians killed were not \"military infrastructure\".\"Israel must halt this escalation,\" he said. \"For the security of our people and their right to life on their land are not subject to negotiation or bargaining.\"The IDF said the incident that prompted its strike on Ansar had taken place in the Ali al-Taher Ridge, a strategic high ground overlooking much of south-eastern Lebanon and one of the war's most fiercely contested positions.A later IDF statement said Ali Samir al-Haj Hassan, who it described as a Hezbollah battalion commander in the Radwan Force unit, had been killed.\"Alongside him, several additional terrorists who were involved in advancing terror attacks against IDF soldiers were eliminated,\" it added.\"The strike was specifically directed at Hassan, who was a lawful target under international law, not at his family members who he used as human shields - hiding alongside them inside the military HQ,\" the IDF said.Lebanon was drawn into the war between Israel, the US and Iran on 2 March, when Hezbollah - the armed group affiliated with Iran - launched rockets into Israel in retaliation for a strike that killed Iran's supreme leader.Israel responded by launching a bombing campaign across Lebanon and invading a significant part of the country's south. Its forces continue to occupy roughly 5% of Lebanese territory.The June ceasefire framework calls for Hezbollah to disarm, Israeli forces to withdraw gradually from southern Lebanon and the Lebanese army to deploy across the area.Lebanon's President Joseph Aoun condemned the Israeli strikes that had killed an entire family in Ansar and its \"repeated violations\" of the framework, which he said sent a \"clear message\" to the US-led negotiations.Hezbollah was not involved in the agreement and has rejected direct negotiations.\"The Israeli enemy must clearly understand that its attacks, violations and attempts to impose a fait accompli cannot continue, and will be met with an appropriate response in defense of Lebanon, its people, sovereignty and national dignity,\" the group said in a statement on Saturday.The IDF says it will continue to remove threats to Israeli citizens and soldiers operating in the security zone - the 10km-deep military buffer in southern Lebanon occupied by Israel.Despite a reduction in fighting following the June agreements, Israeli strikes and demolitions in southern Lebanon have continued.Lebanese and Israeli negotiators are set to meet for an eighth round of negotiations in September, but a final date has not been set.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-15T16:02:49.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/387d/live/b67daca0-9893-11f1-a7ab-8b30adf0800a.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c4gjygx4gjeo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-08-15-switched-on-pop-s-nate-sloan-and-charlie-harding-love-fresh-",
      "title": "Switched on Pop’s Nate Sloan and Charlie Harding love fresh vegetables and guitar pedals",
      "summary": "They’re the Lennon–McCartney of overanalyzing pop music. | Image: Daniel Randall\t\n\nAs if you needed more reason to love Carly Rae Jepsen's \"Call Me Maybe\" beyond its pop perfection, it is also, according to lore, the genesis for Switched on Pop, one of the best music podcasts out there. Cohosts Nate Sloan and Charlie Harding obsessively dissect pop songs, from the theory behind their musical choic",
      "body": "As if you needed more reason to love Carly Rae Jepsen’s “Call Me Maybe” beyond its pop perfection, it is also, according to lore, the genesis for Switched on Pop, one of the best music podcasts out there. Cohosts Nate Sloan and Charlie Harding obsessively dissect pop songs, from the theory behind their musical choices to their production techniques. If you’re a student of pop music, then this is a must-listen podcast.\nSloan is a composer and professor of musicology at USC Thornton School of Music, where he researches pop, but also jazz and even Tin Pan Alley. Harding is a songwriter, music journalist, and a professor as well. Their combined knowledge is near encyclopedic. Whether they’re talking about text painting in Justin Timberlake’s “Can’t Stop the Feeling!” or Taryn Southern’s AI experiments, the two reveal the rich tapestry of even the most simple-seeming songs.\nNow, after 12 years and nearly 500 episodes, the show recently made the leap to video on Netflix, featuring guests like Aaron Dessner (The National), Trevor Horn (The Buggles, Yes), and Cypress Hill. Sloan and Harding told us about the importance of the metronome, fresh vegetables, and what seems to be a slightly problematic relationship with guitar pedals.\nSwitched on Pop is a production of New York Magazine’s Vulture and the Vox Media Podcast Network.\nWhat is your most indispensable tool?\nCharlie Harding: The Martin D-28 is the platonic idea of both what a guitar sounds like and, I’m ashamed to admit how much this matters to me, how a guitar looks. You’ve heard it on “Blackbird,” “Wish You Were Here,” and “Big Yellow Taxi.” I found one from 1970 sitting in an antique shop in Maine. But I’m still waiting for it to produce a timeless classic for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;\nNate Sloan: Sibelius music notation software. Whenever I’m analyzing a song, I don’t truly understand it until I transcribe its melody, harmony, and rhythm.\nWhich is the most underappreciated?\nCH: Keyboard shortcuts and the app Rectangle save me hours of my life every year by doing the mundane task of repositioning windows on my computer. And they also make me feel like I’m mission control on some kind of spaceship.&nbsp;\nWhat is the first app you install on a new phone or computer?\nNS: AudioHijack for grabbing audio.\nWhat is one thing you wish you could change about your phone?\nCH: This is very nitpicky, but it drives me mad. I extensively use focus modes on iOS to help keep away distractions. But I also need people in my family to be able to reach me, so they get exceptions to the focus modes. However, that means any group messaging chain with just one family member blows up my notifications when I’m presenting to a lecture hall full of songwriting students. This means I’m urgently notified every time my recently retired father catches a fish. I am happy for my dad, but I’d be more happy to find out after 5 pm when I’m off&nbsp; “do not disturb.” If this makes me sound like a curmudgeon, I am.&nbsp;\nNS: I wish I could strum it like a guitar.\nWhat sites do you have pinned to your tab bar?\nHow many tabs do you have open right now?\nCH: 92… but they are nicely organized into groups that only I understand.\nNS: Two. I used to have hundreds open, but it was killing my attention span, so I now make a point of constantly closing them.\nWhich social media platform do you use the most (if any)?\nWhat is your happy place online?\nCH: Browsing Sweetwater for variations of guitar pedals that I already own.&nbsp;\nNS: Watching ukulele tutorials on YouTube.\nWhat is your favorite gadget you’ve ever owned?\nCH: The Dato DUO synthesizer and Dato DRUM drum machine. They are the easiest way to learn to program a synthesizer, and you can’t make a bad sound. My kids jam on them every night. It’s turned them into Brooklyn DJs, unfortunately.&nbsp;\nNS: The iPod that I traveled around the world with in 2006.&nbsp;\nWhich was the most disappointing?\nWhat game do you have the fondest memories of?\nWhich tech trend do you wish would go away?\nCH: AI music slop, full stop.&nbsp;\nWhat is one thing you wish you had created?\nCH: In 2007 I pitched my college friends on creating an app that would allow you to legally hitchhike in other people’s cars. We started a tea company instead, which was ultimately less of a liability.\nWhat creation are you most proud of?\nCH: Switched On Pop. My wife read this and said I should add my children.&nbsp;\nNS: Coining the term “pop drop” to describe the instrumental postchorus in a pop song.\nWhich are you least proud of?\nCH: My wife read this and said I should say my guitar pedal collection.&nbsp;\nNS: Getting dunked on by YouTuber Rick Beato after calling the Jonas Brothers “stupefyingly funky” in a New York Times article.\nWhat’s the best piece of advice you’ve ever received?\nCH: You can never have too many guitar pedals.&nbsp;\nNS: Have a short memory.\nWhat is your current obsession?\nNS: LeBron James signing with the 76ers.\nWhat do you do when you need to focus?\nNS: Step away from the computer, take a walk, and start recording a voicenote about what I’m trying to accomplish.\nWhat do you do when you’re feeling stuck?\nWhen was the last time you went somewhere without your phone?\nNS: Whenever I go to the gym.\nWhat’s the last piece of physical media you bought?\nCH: The History of Sound by Ben Shattuck.\nNS: Vinyl copy of the Jeff Parker IVTet.\nWhat do you think is worth splurging on?\nCH: My guitar pedals. And my beautiful wife.&nbsp;\nWhat would the tagline for your biopic be?\nCH: He is embarrassed there is a biopic about him.\nNS: One man dared to overanalyze pop music, taking on BTS Army, Madonna stans, and the&nbsp; Little Monsters, and living to tell the tale.&nbsp;&nbsp;\nWhat’s the last GIF or meme you used?",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Entertainment",
        "Interview",
        "Music",
        "Report"
      ],
      "author": "Terrence O’Brien",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-15T15:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Switched_On_Pop_-_Photo_by_Daniel_Randall.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,9.0320477229517,100,49.177891169102",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/report/980288/switched-on-pop-nate-sloan-charlie-harding-podcast-netflix-interview",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-08-15-star-wars-ahsoka-season-2-and-starfighter-get-teased-at-d23",
      "title": "Star Wars: Ahsoka season 2 and Starfighter get teased at D23",
      "summary": "Season two of Star Wars: Ahsoka is still months away, but Lucasfilm still took the opportunity to tease it a bit at D23. The company dropped the first trailer for the new season ahead of its January 20th, 2027 debut. The clip shows a darker, witchcraft-filled take on the Star Wars universe, with Grand Admiral Thrawn (blue skin, red eyes, and an unsettlingly calm demeanor) threatening to thrust the",
      "body": "Season two of Star Wars: Ahsoka is still months away, but Lucasfilm still took the opportunity to tease it a bit at D23. The company dropped the first trailer for the new season ahead of its January 20th, 2027 debut. The clip shows a darker, witchcraft-filled take on the Star Wars universe, with Grand Admiral Thrawn (blue skin, red eyes, and an unsettlingly calm demeanor) threatening to thrust the galaxy back into large-scale war after the fall of the Empire.\nRyan Gosling also took the stage at D23 to tease the upcoming Star Wars: Starfighter movie. Unfortunately, while some footage was shown, it was exclusive to those in the room, and Lucasfilm hasn’t officially released it online. There have been a few supposed leaks posted on social media, but we haven’t been able to verify their authenticity. However, it likely won’t be too long before the general public gets a look, with Star Wars: Starfighter scheduled to hit theaters on May 28th, 2027, starring Matt Smith, Aaron Pierre, Mia Goth, and more alongside Gosling.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Entertainment",
        "Film",
        "News",
        "Star Wars",
        "TV Shows"
      ],
