    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-11-palace-co-owner-spoke-to-mcneil-s-partner-before-move",
      "title": "Palace co-owner spoke to McNeil's partner before move",
      "summary": "Crystal Palace co-owner and chairman Steve Parish contacts Dwight McNeil's partner before the midfielder's move to the club.",
      "body": "Crystal Palace co-owner and chairman Steve Parish contacted Dwight McNeil's partner before the midfielder's move to the club on Tuesday.McNeil has completed a move to Palace and signed a four-year contract in a swap deal that involved Brennan Johnson moving to Everton.McNeil, 26, nearly moved to Selhurst Park in the January transfer window when Palace agreed a loan-to-buy deal.That deal would have turned into a £20m permanent move, only for the transfer to fall through when the required paperwork could not be completed in time, even though McNeil had already passed a medical.After the deal fell through, McNeil's partner Megan Sharpley was critical of Palace, writing on Instagram: \"We live in a world where everyone is aware of how big a problem mental health is.\"So, in football, why do we find it acceptable because these young men are on a lot of money and that's OK to mess and toy with their mental health and that it's just part of the job?\"To have something promised to you, to have been dragged along on an emotional rollercoaster and to be toyed with until the final minute. For that to be torn away from you at the last second with absolutely no explanation has hurt more than I can say.\"BBC Sport understands that Parish reached out to smooth things over before this week's deal was completed.McNeil joined Everton from Burnley for £20m in July 2022 and scored 15 goals in 128 games for the Toffees.Palace have won the FA Cup, Community Shield and Uefa Conference League in the last two seasons.\"The lads have had some great success over the last two seasons. It's something that I want to be a part of, so I'm really looking forward to getting started,\" McNeil said.Parish said McNeil is \"a player with proven ability in the Premier League\".Johnson signed for Palace in a £35m deal in January but struggled to find form and made 26 appearances in all competitions without scoring.The Wales winger joined Tottenham Hotspur from Nottingham Forest for a fee in excess of £45m in 2023 and scored their winning goal in the Europa League final last May before moving to the Eagles.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-11T12:07:28.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/f594/live/593f4ff0-9580-11f1-8636-978a48bd79b6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cwyjxxg5ze5o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-08-11-joby-flexes-military-muscle-with-500-million-defense-acquisi",
      "title": "Joby flexes military muscle with $500 million defense acquisition",
      "summary": "Joby flight at JFK airport. | Image: The Verge\t\n\nJoby Aviation announced it was acquiring Dayton, Ohio-based defense firm Resonant Sciences in a $500 million deal, in a bid by the electric aircraft company to expand further into the military industrial complex. \nJoby says it expects to finance the deal with $450 million in cash and $50 million in equity. After the deal closes, which is expected in",
      "body": "Joby Aviation announced it was acquiring Dayton, Ohio-based defense firm Resonant Sciences in a $500 million deal, in a bid by the electric aircraft company to expand further into the military industrial complex.\nJoby says it expects to finance the deal with $450 million in cash and $50 million in equity. After the deal closes, which is expected in early 2027, Resonant will continue its existing work as Joby’s dedicated defense division, the company said. The commercial division will remain focused on air taxis, the first of which are expected to launch later this year.\nThe deal is the latest example of the fledgling electric aviation industry looking beyond urban air taxis for new revenue streams, especially as rising costs and a protracted regulatory approval process pushes air taxi success further into the future. For many, lucrative military contracts represent too ripe an opportunity to pass up.\nResonant Sciences, which was founded in 2015 and employs around 250 people, develops systems that help aircraft see, communicate, and operate in complex environments, including war zones. Its sensors, antennas, and signal-processing technology to gather information support secure communications and protect against interference, while its low-observability expertise helps make aircraft harder to detect. Resonant’s products are already qualified across more than 20 commercial and defense airframes. And many of the firm’s employees have military clearance due to the classified nature of their work.\nJoby says the acquisition will allow it to combine its propulsion and autonomy tech with Resonant’s radio frequency, sensing, and mission systems capabilities. The company says it intends to design and build stealth aircraft using its newly acquired systems.\nJoby has long had ties to the military, stretching back to 2016 when the company participated in the Department of Defense’s Defense Innovation Unit project. In 2020, Joby became the first eVTOL developer to secure an airworthiness approval and flight clearance certificate from the US Air Force for government-led testing. And in 2023, it delivered its first aircraft to Edwards Air Force Base for testing.\nElectric aircraft developers are increasingly looking to military projects to help shore up their cash-intensive business. Air taxis have yet to obtain the necessary government approvals in most markets that would allow them to launch their commercial service and bring in more money. As such, most of these companies, Joby included, regularly report steep quarterly losses. Last week, Joby reported a net loss of $245.4 million in the second quarter of 2026 on $38.6 million in revenue.\nGetting more military contracts is increasingly becoming just as important as launching a viable air taxi service. Last month, San Jose-based Archer Aviation, one of Joby’s main rivals, unveiled a new aircraft it codeveloped with defense technology company Anduril. Consolidation is also an emerging theme. Boeing said yesterday it was selling three of its eVTOL subsidiaries to Archer as part of a new blockbuster deal.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Aviation",
        "Business",
        "News",
        "Transportation"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew J. Hawkins",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-11T12:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/06/DSC01507.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.806933475316,100,78.386133049368",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/transportation/977533/joby-resonant-sciences-acquisition-evtol",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-11-bath-in-serious-discussions-to-play-in-reading",
      "title": "Bath in 'serious discussions' to play in Reading",
      "summary": "Reading owner Rob Couhig says the club have had \"serious discussions\" with Bath about the Prem Rugby club playing at their stadium.",
      "body": "Bath's games against Harlequins on the weekend of 15 May and Saracens on 5 June are scheduled to be moved, and if the club were to reach the Prem semi-finals and secure a home tie next June, that fixture would also be played away from the Rec.The club's owners say the plans are being accelerated to ensure the redevelopment is completed for the start of the 2028-29 Prem campaign.Reading's final home game of the upcoming season is against Sheffield Wednesday on 1 May. However, they would be due to host another match that month if they reach the League One play-offs.Their ground has been regularly used for rugby in the past, having been the home of London Irish between 2000 to 2020.Couhig highlighted a number of changes and upgrades which have been made at the SCL Stadium this summer - including to the stands and a new pitch which has been laid - as factors that make a possible agreement with Bath feasible.\"This pitch is built in a way that it could handle that and recover very quickly [from rugby],\" Couhig said.\"Our football team is the first priority tenant and we would make sure it is handled the right way.\"He added that any money generated would be reinvested in the football team.\"Let's say we did something with Bath Rugby, then we'd be allowed additional money under the SCMP [salary cost management protocol] to spend on our football players,\" he said.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-11T11:50:02.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/7607/live/ab7b3840-9569-11f1-8b14-5d44448d174a.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/rugby-union/articles/cx2jrge11rwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-11-how-30m-charles-became-northern-ireland-s-most-expensive-foo",
      "title": "How £30m Charles became Northern Ireland's most expensive footballer",
      "summary": "After he became Northern Ireland's most expensive player, BBC Sport NI looks at the rise of Shea Charles, from Manchester City's academy to Fulham's £30m man.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, From coming through at Manchester City's academy, Shea Charles has become the most expensive Northern Ireland player in historyByAndy GrayBBC Sport NI Journalist\"Shea has been so, so impressive during my time working with him. I have never seen anyone better on the ball than this kid.\"It was in 2021 that the Northern Ireland youth coach Gerard Lyttle said he had never seen a youngster as comfortable on the ball as Shea Charles.Back then, the teenager was still playing at centre-back and had yet to make a senior appearance for club or country, but he was standing out to his coaches.Five years later, midfielder Charles has become the most expensive Northern Ireland player in history after he completed a £30m transfer from Southampton to Premier League side Fulham.From coming through at Manchester City, to becoming one of Michael O'Neill's most important players on the international scene, the 22-year-old has taken the long way round to a top-flight return.Unexpected chance and learning from RodriFrom when he joined Manchester City's academy as a seven-year-old, Charles' potential was clear and he quickly progressed through the ranks.His breakout year came in the 2021-22 season, when he helped City win the Premier League 2 title as an 18-year-old playing alongside the likes of Cole Palmer, Morgan Rogers and Nico O'Reilly.He was handed a first Northern Ireland call-up for June's Nations League window in 2022, and he made his international debut as a late substitute against Greece before he had even made a senior appearance at club level.It was somewhat unexpected given his lack of experience, but it showcased the belief that then manager Ian Baraclough showed in Charles.\"I hope I can just push on from here and get more caps. It was a great feeling to make my debut,\" Charles said at the time.\"I didn't expect to be playing in this game, but I will take every chance as it comes.\"Charles has certainly done that, establishing himself as a key player for Michael O'Neill, who returned as manager later in 2022 to replace Baraclough.In fact, Charles has only missed three matches since then. One was for a suspension following a red card against Slovenia, while his absence with a hamstring injury was notable in the closing World Cup qualifiers with Slovakia and Luxembourg in November last year.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Shea Charles is the subject of two of the three biggest transfer fees ever paid for a Northern Ireland internationalFor Lyttle, having worked with Charles at age-group level with Northern Ireland, there was never any doubting his potential.\"Honestly, he is so calm, I actually haven't seen him get out of second gear yet and that is no joke - there is even more in him,\" Lyttle added in his 2021 interview with BBC Sport.\"He plays with such a calmness, sees a pass, breaks lines, is technically very good, can hit long and short and can compete physically. He is just a proper ball-playing centre-back and a real good kid as well.\"As he goes up the levels he needs to keep progressing, but from what I've seen of young Shea he definitely has all the attributes.\"It helped that Charles was learning from some of the best in the world. At Manchester City he had been training with the likes of Rodri and Fernandinho and was finally handed a Premier League chance in May 2023, by which stage he had six international caps under his belt.Pep Guardiola's side had already wrapped up the Premier League title, and Charles made his City debut as a second-half substitute against Brentford on the final day of the season.\"When we train with the first team the standard is so high and we are always learning, every day,\" Charles said in 2022.\"With the likes of Rodri and Fernandinho, it is tough but enjoyable.\"'I back myself against anyone'While learning from the best players in the world was beneficial, it did not offer a natural path of progression to the first team at Etihad Stadium.So, a month after making his City debut, Charles signed a four-year contract with Southampton and subsequently made 32 appearances as Saints won promotion back to the Premier League.Instead of contributing in his club's top-flight return, his development continued with a loan spell at Sheffield Wednesday, where he became a fan favourite and drew links to Arsenal.\"He's a young, high-potential player – the pity is that he is not our player,\" said Wednesday's manager at the time, Danny Rohl, about his loan star during his early days at Hillsborough.Midway through his season with the Owls, Charles was handed the Northern Ireland armband by O'Neill just 10 days after his 21st birthday, the youngest player to captain the country since Terry Neill in 1963.\"Shea has got the attributes to play at the highest level of the game, and the temperament to do so, and that is what you look for as a captain,\" O'Neill said at the time.\"Whether he has the armband or not, he is going to be a leader on the pitch.\"Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, With his most prolific season in front of goal, Charles scored the winner as Southampton beat Arsenal to reach the FA Cup semi-finals last seasonWith Southampton back in the Championship after their relegation, Charles returned to St Mary's last season.He contributed some big moments and chipped in with his biggest return in front of goal with six strikes in the campaign.Moments such as his winner against eventual Premier League champions Arsenal in the FA Cup quarter-finals, or a goal against Liverpool at Anfield in the EFL Cup, will live long in the memory on the south coast.He also netted in the Championship play-off semi-final win over Middlesbrough with a goal that would have taken the Saints to Wembley, only for the club to be denied at shot at the top flight after the Spygate drama.While Southampton missed out on the Premier League, O'Neill was adamant that Charles has what it takes to play in the top flight. It was a matter of when, not if.\"Shea can play in the Premier League, there's no doubt about that,\" said Charles' international boss before June's friendlies with Guinea and France.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Shea Charles stood out against the strong sides of France and Italy this yearIt was in the France game that Charles showed he belonged among the elite players, not looking out of place against a side who went on to be World Cup semi-finalists.It was the same against Italy in March's World Cup play-off, where he was arguably the best player on the pitch against Sandro Tonali and Nicolo Barella despite the 2-0 defeat which ended NI's dreams of reaching the finals.\"I back myself against anyone,\" was the player's own assessment after that game.Charles was strongly linked with a move to Leeds United, but instead Fulham will be his destination as he looks to take the next step in his career under former Real Madrid and Liverpool defender Alvaro Arbeloa, now head coach of the London side.With 35 international caps under his belt and a host of clutch moments to his name at the age of 22, Charles could be one of the surprises of the season as he aims to have a breakout Premier League campaign at Craven Cottage.More on this story",
