    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-11-trump-hid-in-catering-truck-in-secret-plane-swap-over-iran-t",
      "title": "Trump hid in catering truck in secret plane swap over Iran threat, reports say",
      "summary": "The US president was reportedly part of a ruse where reporters were falsely told he was with them on Air Force One leaving a Nato summit in Turkey.",
      "body": "Figure caption, Watch: President Trump secretly switches planes to avoid Iran threatDonald Trump secretly swapped planes as he left a Nato summit in Turkey last month, as part of an elaborate ruse in response to a possible Iranian threat, US media report.The US president boarded Air Force One in view of television cameras before being  transported to a military aircraft with the help of a catering truck, the Washington Post said. Journalists and some White House staff on board did not know he had been smuggled out. Officials told the BBC's US partner CBS News that the US had detected a credible Iranian threat to fire a missile at the plane. The White House has not confirmed the secret switch.Trump had earlier said he was Iran's \"number one target\" during the summit in Ankara. The clandestine manoeuvre came a day after the US renewed military strikes on Iran following a breakdown of negotiations between Washington and Tehran to end the war.Trump had arrived in Turkey aboard a newer Boeing 747-8 donated by Qatar, but announced that he would leave on the old Air Force One \"for old time's sake\".On 8 July, footage showed him boarding the presidential jet at Ankara airport. But according to the Washington Post, he was then secretly taken to a smaller C-32A military aircraft - a modified Boeing 757 often used by the vice-president - via a catering truck which had been elevated to plane level on the opposite side to where he had boarded.Defence Secretary Pete Hegseth boarded the C-32A separately using external stairs in an effort to make the flight appear normal, according to the newspaper.Journalists and some White House staff boarded Air Force One and were reportedly told to close their window shades. They were unaware the president was no longer on board.Trump then flew to the UK en route to the US, where he reboarded the old Air Force One and was seen descending from the aircraft after it landed at RAF Mildenhall. It is unclear how he moved from the C-32A back to the legacy jet. Image source, ReutersImage caption, He then boarded the Qatar-gifted jet to fly from the UK to the USThe president then transferred to the new Qatari-donated Air Force One to travel back to Washington. During that flight, Trump spoke to reporters on board where he noted the threats posed by Iran but did not mention the earlier plane swap.Asked why reporters had been told to lower the window shades on the plane from Turkey, Trump said: \"Because you're probably on a dangerous flight because of the sleazebags we have to deal with.\"He said he had a \"threat all the time\" on his life, and that he is \"number one\" on Iran's \"list\".\"But if I go, you go, right?\", he said, adding: \"Perhaps someday you want to change professions.\"Image source, ReutersImage caption, Trump spoke about the threat to him from Iran after boarding the presidential jet given to him by QatarWhite House communications director Steven Cheung did not directly confirm or deny the reports, but said the new Air Force One was a \"state-of-the-art aircraft\" fitted with security measures to protect the president and his staff.US intelligence had previously warned of a specific threat of an attack on Trump or his aircraft, according to earlier reporting by CBS News.The New York Times previously reported on security concerns regarding the Qatari-gifted jet, including that the Secret Service had urged Trump to switch planes on his return from the Nato summit.Several of the publication's journalists were summoned to testify under oath in response. The Justice Department subsequently told the BBC that it was investigating illegal leaks.The US has spent months retrofitting the $400m Boeing 747-8 to prepare it for the president. Older Air Force One planes have laser technology to blind incoming missiles, sources have told CBS News. It is unclear what systems the Qatari jet has.\"The new Air Force One is perfectly safe for the President's travels, but will receive additional upgrades and enhancements in the fall which will take approximately one month to complete,\" White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt said in a statement to CBS News.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-11T09:58:45.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7f69/live/2dd49110-956f-11f1-b2ab-0dd01740f9f6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm2g90vvy62o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-11-russia-using-north-korean-missiles-to-strike-ukraine-zelensk",
      "title": "Russia using North Korean missiles to strike Ukraine, Zelensky says",
      "summary": "Ukraine's president says strikes in Zaporizhzhia - which killed at least six and injured 19 - involved ballistic missiles from Russia's ally.",
      "body": "Ukraine and Russia have continued striking each other overnight, with Volodymyr Zelensky accusing Moscow of using North Korean missiles.The Ukrainian president said strikes in Zaporizhzhia - which killed at least six and injured at least 19 - involved ballistic missiles from Russia's ally.In Voronezh, in southern Russia, warehouses belonging to the online retailer, Wildberries, were hit by Ukraine. The company, known as Russia's Amazon, has been repeatedly targeted in recent weeks.The attacks come a day after at least 13 people were killed, including one child, in Ukrainian drone strikes on the Russian republic of Tatarstan, more than 1,100 km (680 miles) from the Ukrainian border.In a video address on Monday evening, Zelensky said \"for the first time\" Russia \"cannot wage war without reinforcements from North Korea\".\"The more North Korean strikes there are here in Ukraine, in Europe, the more their missiles and soldiers are used, the more they correct their shortcomings and blind spots\", Zelensky said, adding that this poses a danger to the rest of the world.In his address, Zelensky also hailed a successful air strike on a Russian petrochemical plant in western Siberia on Monday as a \"very significant achievement\" by the Ukrainian military.He promised \"more Ukrainian deep strikes, which should demonstrate to Russia and the Russians that peace is necessary\".It is not the first time Zelensky has accused Russia of receiving military assistance from North Korea.He warned over the weekend that as many as 50,000 North Korean soldiers were being sent to fight with Russian forces. Zaporizhzhia Regional Governor Ivan Fedorov posted photos of vehicles and buildings on fire, saying Moscow had struck the city using missiles and guided bombs. In addition to those killed, at least 19 others were injured One person was injured in strikes on Kyiv, where air raid sirens sounded twice during the night. Explosions could be heard on the outskirts of the capital, triggering fires.Fedorov said industrial facilities, infrastructure and non-residential buildings in Zaporizhzhia were hit in the Russian attack.Ukrainian officials say Russia fired 120 drones overnight and a range of different missiles, but for the first time they have not given a number of missiles fired or intercepted.It follows an Air Force statement on Monday urging the media not to write \"sensationalised headlines\" or \"spread pessimism\" about the shortage of air defence interceptors.In Kyiv, a strike damaged a hospital, but officials say staff and patients were sheltering. Meanwhile, strikes on substations in Kherson and Kharkiv resulted in power outages.Two women have been injured in a Russian drone strike on Sumy, according to Sergiy Kryvosheenko, head of the Sumy City Military Administration.The women, aged 52 and 66, were treated at the scene, he says in a post on Telegram.The strikes also damaged civilian vehicles, warehouse buildings and infrastructure facilities, he adds.Separately, there have been reports of a fire and explosions at a logistics centre of Russia's biggest online retailer, Wildberries, in the Voronezh region.Wildberries has not yet confirmed the attack, but said that its facilities in the region, which borders Ukraine, have been evacuated.In July, Ukrainian drone attacks on several retail warehouses belonging to Wildberries disrupted the operations of a large number of small and medium-sized Russian businesses.Kyiv said at the time it was targeting logistics hubs used by Russians to buy military equipment, and disrupting supplies to the Russian army.The attack on Tatarstan this week was one of the deadliest single attacks on Russia since the start of the full-scale war in Ukraine in 2022.At least 75 people were injured in the strikes, with Ukraine's Armed Forces saying it had hit the city's Tanevo oil refinery.Verified images on social media showed large plumes of smoke and flames rising into the sky in daytime, with air raid sirens howling in the background.The Uzkbek consulate in Tatarstan said seven of those killed were citizens of Uzbekistan.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-11T09:39:58.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7862/live/6d64a760-953e-11f1-ba5b-b1a600ddfa74.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c151dpzwnvxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-11-call-with-no-10-gives-us-hope-pc-harper-s-mother-says",
      "title": "Call with No 10 gives us hope, PC Harper's mother says",
      "summary": "Debbie Adlam says she does not want a \"special case\" for her son, but for killers of emergency workers to be exempt.",
      "body": "Cole and Bowers were sentenced to 13 years for manslaughter in 2020. Under changes to the Sentencing Act made this year, they will become eligible for release from custody next January.They are in a category of prisoners - those who have committed certain serious violent or sexual crimes - who until now would have had to serve at least two-thirds of their sentence in jail before being considered for release.Downing Street had earlier warned it would not be possible to exclude \"one or two offenders\" convicted of manslaughter from the early release scheme.However, the BBC has been told that Burnham has now instructed his justice secretary to find a way to exclude Cole and Bowers from the scheme designed to ease prison overcrowding.In a later statement, the PM said he was \"increasingly confident\" that work to increase space in the prison system \"will allow us to reduce the number of people released early\".He added: \"And, in the case of the most serious offenders like those convicted of the killing of PC [Andrew] Harper, prevent their early release altogether.\"Adlam said the conflicting reports had been making her \"more and more angry\" and the uncertainty had left her family \"in turmoil\".Of the phone call she received, she said. \"I think the fact it's actually come from Downing Street, I've got to try and have some faith in it, but at the same time I've got to protect my own feelings.\"Thames Valley Police officer PC Harper, 28, from Wallingford, Oxfordshire, died in 2019 when he was dragged behind the getaway car of three teenagers who had fled the scene of a quad bike theft in Berkshire.Adlam said: \"Everybody is watching this. This is obviously extremely personal to us but it's to do with the country as a whole really. This is really vital to get right, it feels like a hash, like a rush job.\"Speaking about Cole and Bowers, she added: \"They weren't given extreme sentences and I'm only asking for them to do what they were given, I'm not asking for anything more than they were sentenced.\"Aileen O'Connor, chairman of Thames Valley Police Federation, said the reports that Burnham would look to exclude Cole and Bowers from the early release scheme, gave her a \"little glimmer of hope\".She told BBC Radio 4's Today programme: \"Police officers up and down this country run towards danger, they put themselves on the line every single day and Andrew Harper paid the ultimate sacrifice for the job that he loved and the badge he wore.\"We need to see the government protecting us because we're getting battered all over the show every single day.\"Housing, Communities and Local Government Secretary Angela Rayner said the government was still looking at all options around the prisons crisis and preventing the early release of PC Harper's killers.She told BBC Breakfast on Tuesday: \"The trauma that PC Harper's family has been through, and other victims who are watching this crisis unfold in our prisons, is unacceptable.\"The prime minister has said that those serious offenders, like the ones that killed PC Harper, he doesn't want to see them released early and he has tasked the justice secretary to look at that and to come to parliament in September so that we can do everything we possibly can to stop that from happening.\"Last week, PC Harper's widow, Lissie Harper, said she \"will not give up\" and hoped people would share a petition calling for his killers to be denied early release from prison.She said: \"Hopefully we can make the current government come to their senses and realise we cannot put people like this back on our streets.\"Justice should mean justice and serving the sentence given.\"More than 900,000 people have signed the petition set up by Thames Valley Police Federation, which calls the decision for Cole and Bowers to be eligible for early release an \"insult\".",
