    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-10-ex-cambridge-professor-jason-arday-cancels-live-event-after-",
      "title": "Ex-Cambridge professor Jason Arday cancels live event after plagiarism row",
      "summary": "One talk by Arday is \"cancelled at the request of the speaker\" and others are removed from listings.",
      "body": "In another development, BBC News has learned that Arday resigned on Sunday as a publication director and trustee of the British Sociological Association (BSA), the national body for sociology academics.The BSA, to which Arday gave a lecture earlier this year claiming black academics were being targeted in \"evil\" ways, told the BBC it would shortly amend its website to reflect the change.It was one of multiple roles Arday held in various organisations.The NHS Race and Health Observatory - which studies racial and ethnic inequality in health - lists him as a member of its \"academic reference advisory group\", citing his now-out-of-date Cambridge title. Arday was also named as a fellow of the Royal Society of Arts by Cambridge when it appointed him.Neither organisation replied to the BBC's request for clarification on whether they remained current roles.Arday also sat on ITV's Cultural Advisory Council and as an expert the Welsh Government's anti-racism external accountability group, but both roles ended earlier this year.When asked by the BBC, Advance HE declined to confirm whether Arday remains a senior fellow of its formerly named Higher Education Academy, citing data protection law. The senior fellowship is awarded to individuals who \"lead or influence the learning and teaching practices of others\".The Runnymede Trust think tank, for whom Arday was a trustee until 2024, released a statement defending him last week and condemning \"racialised, hyper-aggressive media scrutiny\".The publisher of Arday's memoir Great and Unfortunate Things is going ahead with its release in the US on Tuesday and in the UK on 27 August as planned.The book tells the story of his life from being non-verbal until the age of 11 and unable to read or write until 18, then going through a series of \"improbable triumphs and heartbreaking setbacks\", before being appointed professor of sociology of education at Cambridge.However, he has been accused of plagiarism in his PhD thesis, and questions have been raised about the reliability of parts of his life story, including that he ran 600 miles in six days and that he had raised £5.5m for charity.On Wednesday, the University of Cambridge said it had launched an investigation \"following new information\" about his \"academic qualifications and honorary appointments\".Announcing his resignation on the same day, Arday said the \"personal cost\" of the scrutiny had become \"too great\".He said: \"While criticism is an inevitable part of academic life, what I have experienced has gone far beyond scholarly disagreement.\"The relentless accusations, speculation and public commentary have taken a profound toll on me and on those I love.\"He stressed that his resignation should not \"be mistaken for an acceptance of the narratives that have surrounded me\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-10T14:58:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/db37/live/09edcf80-94a9-11f1-afe9-fb1a837ec5d9.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cd7lj3703epo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-08-10-kinney-drugs-pulls-back-ai-phone-assistant-after-hundreds-of",
      "title": "Kinney Drugs pulls back AI phone assistant after hundreds of customer complaints",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.wcax.com/2026/08/07/kinney-drugs-pulls-back-ai-phone-assistant-after-hundreds-customer-complaints/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49244569\nPoints: 98\n# Comments: 102",
      "body": "BURLINGTON, Vt. (WCAX) - Kinney Drugs is scaling back its AI assistant after customers reported incoherent calls, wrong dosages, and missed prescription notifications.Kinney Drugs first introduced Burt, an AI assistant named after the pharmacy chain’s founder, back in May. The bot’s role was to communicate with patients about prescriptions and refills. But customers quickly reported a number of issues with the tech, as first reported by VTDigger.“But one thing I want to point out is getting privacy and security right does not mean we got the experience right. We did not, and we own that,” Kinney Drugs President John Marraffa said.Marraffa says Kinney will return to its old model for incoming patient calls — the classic touch-tone phone system. Customers also raised concerns about data privacy — worried their personal health information could be exposed through an AI platform. Marraffa says Burt is fully HIPAA compliant and that the AI is not open-source and does not generate or manipulate data. Kinney will keep Burt for outbound communications like prescription refill texts, but patients must opt in to receive them.Copyright 2026 WCAX. All rights reserved.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "kotaKat",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-10T14:56:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://gray-wcax-prod.gtv-cdn.com/resizer/v2/QEV2FCRJFVE4HN5LGUQU3IWK5U.png?auth=2f0b57d18eb3d3dbb7bc5fb4d2610e3390e058794706a44bf2b74201e08cff23&width=1200&height=600&smart=true",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.wcax.com/2026/08/07/kinney-drugs-pulls-back-ai-phone-assistant-after-hundreds-customer-complaints/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-10-should-liverpool-fans-be-worried-about-iraola-s-fitness-comm",
      "title": "Should Liverpool fans be worried about Iraola's fitness comments?",
      "summary": "Should Liverpool fans be worried about Andoni Iraola's comments concerning the squad's fitness?",
      "body": "For the second friendly in a row, Andoni Iraola's Liverpool threw away a two-goal lead and went on to lose the game.In Chicago on 2 August, new Liverpool manager Iraola said the 4-2 friendly defeat against Leeds was useful as he \"learned a lot of things\" about his side.However, at Anfield on Sunday against Monaco, the Spaniard was a little more candid after a 3-2 loss.Two weeks out from the start of the Premier League campaign, Iraola's verdict was that his side had \"work to do\" because they could not sustain the level that he wanted for the duration of a game.Iraola said that his side played a good first 30 minutes but \"they don't have much more, in terms of the levels required to play like this\".For what it's worth, the first half against Leeds and the first 30 minutes against Monaco was the best that Liverpool have looked throughout pre-season. They moved the ball with pace, were direct, pressed with intensity and both Florian Wirtz and Alexander Isak have looked sharp.But as the game progressed and changes were made - Iraola made nine against Leeds and 11 against Monaco - Liverpool have looked like a different team and certainly not for the better. Leeds scored four goals in 25 minutes without reply while Monaco scored three unanswered goals in 44 minutes too. Some of the squad are still working their way back to full fitness after some time off following the World Cup while others such as Conor Bradley, Hugo Ekitike and Giovanni Leoni are sidelined for lengthy periods of time. Uruguay defender Ronald Araujo, who can play at centre-back and right-back, completed a season-long loan move from Barcelona on Monday.On Sunday, skipper Virgil van Dijk said: \"Let's see what the next couple of weeks are going to bring. Any quality we can add to the team will definitely be welcome so let's see.\"But with the squad at his disposal, Iraola is adamant that the general fitness must improve if they are to play the style that he wants. Pablo de la Torre, one of Iraola's assistants, has also made the move to Liverpool and was previously the fitness coach at Bournemouth.Across the small sample size of four games, there have been enough signs of Liverpool playing in a busy manner in the early phases of games and that intensity was evident to see during an open training session in Chicago, where Iraola used phrases like \"quicker, quicker\" repeatedly. One player told BBC Sport how he felt sick after the first few sessions while the punishment for losing sides in training has been to sprint to the other end of the pitch and back.In Chicago, Milos Kerkez said that Iraola is asking the players to \"play one-on-one all over the pitch, to press high and to always be on the front foot\".Iraola's Bournemouth side ranked first in the Premier League season for direct attacks and third when it came to high turnovers.It is worth noting that it took him 10 games to secure his first win at Bournemouth in the 2023-24 season. Any such run at Liverpool would be unacceptable but you only have to look at the way Bournemouth performed throughout his tenure to see why Iraola persisted with his ways.His comments on Sunday were certainly concerning but he has had less than a month to work with his players and only a week or so with some. The signs are there that this team will entertain but as Iraola pointed out in Chicago, it will take time.\"I would like to say, no, on the first day, we are going to be perfect, everyone will be in place, but probably it is not going to be like this,\" he said, when asked if it would take a while before fully seeing his style of play. \"We will have to do this as quickly as possible.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-10T14:54:09.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/3060/live/83fd9be0-94c5-11f1-9730-59a19ad5a9b7.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cy071ngvnzzo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-10-record-230-people-cross-channel-in-one-small-boat",
      "title": "Record 230 people cross Channel in one small boat",
      "summary": "The Home Office says it shows the \"reckless and dangerous tactics\" used by gangs \"cramming ever greater numbers on to unseaworthy vessels\".",
      "body": "Image source, SNSMImage caption, The large inflatable boat was pictured off the coast of France packed with peopleByDaniel SextonSimon JonesJacob PanonsSouth East and Daniel SandfordUK correspondentA small boat has crossed the English Channel carrying 230 people, the largest number to make the journey in a single vessel.It breaks the previous record set in July, when 165 people made the crossing on a single vessel.The boat arrived in the UK in the early hours of Monday.A picture taken off the coast of northern France showed the inflatable boat was so full many passengers had to dangle their legs in the water as it entered the busy shipping route.A Home Office spokesperson said this latest incident demonstrated the \"reckless and dangerous tactics employed by criminal gangs... who continue to put lives at risk by cramming ever greater numbers of people onto unseaworthy vessels\".French rescuers said the 15m (49ft) boat was \"buckling\" in the water and could have led to a \"tragedy\".The boat was already carrying about 50 people when it was seen heading towards a beach to pick more passengers up, the crew in Berck-sur-Mer added.French maritime officials said 30 migrants were left stranded by the tide during a boarding attempt at Berck beach in northern France. They were brought to safety by maritime and land-based rescue services, the statement from France's Channel and North Sea Maritime Prefecture said.The boat carrying 232 people continued its journey, before two passengers requested assistance and were taken to Calais by a French Navy rescue ship, the statement added.Image source, SNSMImage caption, The 230 people on one boat breaks the previous record from July when 165 people made the crossing on a single vesselThere are signs smugglers have been putting increasing numbers of people into each boat. In 2018, there was an average of seven people per boat - but last year, that had risen to 62.Last Tuesday, 173 migrants had to be rescued from a single boat, which caught fire. Some 258 migrants arrived in the UK in four boats on Saturday, according to Home Office figures, with 423 arriving in five boats on Sunday.However, the number of crossings overall is on course to be lower this year.The provisional total for the number who have made the crossing so far in 2026 is 15,242, which is down by 43%, while the number of boats that have successfully crossed is down by 49%.Last month, the Home Office recorded the lowest number of crossings in July since 2020.Officials think this is partly because they have managed to disrupt the supply of small boats and engines to the people smugglers, with 1,100 seized since Labour came to power.Officials claim that with the sophisticated surveillance and reconnaissance operation now operating over the Channel, \"unintercepted\" beach landings have ended.'Clear demand'All boats that make it across the halfway point in the Channel are met at sea by Border Force, which then brings the migrants safely ashore for processing.There is still clear demand for the crossings as the number of people waiting in camps in northern France has not reduced. At the same time, weather conditions have not been significantly different this year.Officials believe this shows their work to disrupt the smuggling gangs is beginning to show results. Despite this, concerns persist about the numbers still able to make it to the UK.\nHow many people cross the English Channel in small boats?\nBetween 1 January and 8 August 2026, a total of 14,819 people crossed the English Channel by small boat from France. This was down by 43% on the same period the previous year.\nThese crossings have become the most common way for people to be detected entering the UK illegally since 2020.\nNearly all those who arrive by small boat claim asylum. Under international law, this means they are allowed to stay in the country while their asylum application is considered.\nSmall boat arrivals made up 42% of asylum applications between April 2025 to March 2026.\nBoats that arrived in the UK from 9 August 2025 to 8 August 2026 carried an average of 67 people. This has more than doubled since 2021.\nExperts say overcrowding in boats makes crossings riskier. At least 84 people died while attempting to cross the Channel in 2024, according to the United Nations (UN).\nWhen looking at the scale of small boats crossings, the number of these arrivals is about 5% of the size of total immigration into the UK from January 2025 to December 2025.\nThe government has pledged to “smash the gangs” behind these crossings in order to reduce the numbers.\nWho is arriving in the UK on small boats?\nPeople from Eritrea accounted for 18% of all arrivals from April 2025 to March 2026.\nIn the latest figures covering January 2025 to December 2025, at least 2,000 people who arrived by small boat were found to be potential victims of human trafficking or other forms of modern slavery, according to the Home Office.\nHow else do people stay in the UK without permission?\nAnother 4,535 people were detected entering the UK without permission via other methods including hiding in vehicles, travelling on ferries or through airports from April 2025 to March 2026. This was down by 22% on the same period the previous year.\nThere are also individuals who arrive legally, for example via a work or study visa, and then overstay their limit.\nThe total number of people who live in the UK illegally after arriving legally is not known.\nTen people have been sentenced for endangering lives at sea since a new law designed to target people who pilot small boats came into force in January.A spokesperson for the prime minister said: \"We've seen today some appalling images... of young babies being put into these unseaworthy craft... shocking images, and they show the reality behind the ruthless smuggling gangs that are prepared to risk human lives for profit...\"\"As the prime minister has said, we're not complacent and we are bearing down on these crossings, and that's reflected in the joint action that's prevented 47,000 attempted crossings since the election.\"Image source, PA MediaImage caption, The inflatable boat used during the crossing was pictured deflated on the quayside in DoverThe English Channel is one of the most dangerous, and busiest, shipping lanes in the world.Many migrants come from some of the poorest and most chaotic parts of the world, and ask to claim asylum once they are picked up by the UK authorities.On a recent visit to Dover, the prime minister pledged his government would be \"relentless\" in curbing the number of small boat arrivals.Andy Burnham said enforcement officers tackling people-smuggling gangs had been doubled on both sides of the Channel, but \"a system that offers safe routes for people\" needed to be built as well.In April, the UK and France struck a £662m (773m euro) deal aiming to address the issue.The three-year agreement included plans to send riot-trained police to French beaches to tackle violence and \"hostile crowds\", as well as France deploying millions of pounds worth of drones and two helicopters as part of a system to intercept people smugglers and illegal migrants.'Security emergency'On X, Reform UK leader Nigel Farage said: \"At 2.30am this morning a boat carrying 230 people was taken into Dover.\"This is a record number in a single vessel.\"It's yet more proof that this illegal invasion is a national security emergency.\"Earlier on Monday, a Home Office spokesperson said: \"We are making progress, with the number of small boat crossings down this year and the lowest number recorded for the month of July since 2020.\"But we must go further, which is why we have signed a payment-by-results deal with the French to put 40% more boots on the ground on French beaches.\"Follow BBC Kent on Facebook, external, on X, external, on Instagram, external and listen to BBC Radio Kent on Sounds. Send your story ideas to southeasttoday@bbc.co.uk, external or WhatsApp us on 08081 002250.More on this storyRelated internet links",
